From a970bac692ad5cfab79079bbe854d99931ffa2e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:28:21 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 64/94] ASoC: codecs: hiby-hm100: play on S/PDIF with nothing
 plugged in

Stock routes a stream with nothing plugged in to the Spdif target (4):
its HAL maps the speaker device to the spdif path, which powers the
FPGA alone with the analog side down, and its kernel puts reg0 in mode
1, where the FPGA forwards SD to its S/PDIF pin. The HAL encodes the
IEC 60958 biphase-mark cells itself; the FPGA does not.

Add the Spdif target with stock's power-on and power-off order, and
resolve Auto with nothing plugged to it. Every stream now gets a
target in hw_params, so the FPGA, which is the I2S clock provider,
always runs and a stream with no jack no longer stalls the graph. As
stock's hm100_set_spdif_mode, hm100_startup latches whether the stream
is S/PDIF cells (opened while the target is Spdif); such a stream gets
the spdif_table word of its S/PDIF frame rate (16 bytes per frame),
anything else the PCM word of its rate. The word follows the stream
across output switches, and an analog output stays muted while it
carries S/PDIF cells, which would be full-scale noise there. An output
switch with a stream open restarts it with its word, and Spdif is
appended to the Output control so the saved indices keep their
meaning.

